/**
 * LinkedIn content, supplied by hand.
 *
 * There is no way to fetch this automatically. LinkedIn has no public API for a
 * member's posts or certifications — the Marketing and Member Data APIs both
 * require OAuth plus an approved partner application, and scraping the site
 * needs an authenticated session and is against their terms. So nothing here
 * can be generated by a build script the way src/data/github.json is.
 *
 * What LinkedIn *does* support officially is post embedding, which is what the
 * `posts` array below uses.
 *
 * ── Adding a post ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * 1. Open the post on LinkedIn (it must be public).
 * 2. Click the "..." menu on the post → "Embed this post".
 * 3. Copy the `src` URL out of the iframe snippet it gives you. It looks like
 *    https://www.linkedin.com/embed/feed/update/urn:li:share:7123456789012345678
 * 4. Add an entry below. `title` and `date` are yours to write — they are shown
 *    as a caption above the embed so the section still reads when the iframe is
 *    slow or blocked.
 *
 * ── Adding certifications ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Fill in `certifications` in src/data/timeline.js. Two ways to get the list:
 *   · Type them out — name, issuer, date, credential ID and URL.
 *   · Or export them: LinkedIn → Settings → Data privacy → Get a copy of your
 *     data → tick "Certifications" → the archive contains Certifications.csv,
 *     which has exactly those columns.
 * Drop the certificate images into public/Certificates/ and reference them with
 * the `image` field to show the certificate itself.
 */

export const linkedinProfile = 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/priyanshu-chaurasia-326979335/'

/**
 * A share URL carries the activity id you need:
 *   .../posts/<slug>-activity-<ACTIVITY_ID>-<hash>?...
 * The embed is that id in a URN. Everything after the id in a share link is
 * tracking and can be dropped.
 *
 * The ids are snowflake-style — the high 41 bits are a unix millisecond
 * timestamp — which is where the dates below come from, so they cannot drift
 * out of sync with the posts they label.
 */
const embed = (activityId) =>
  `https://www.linkedin.com/embed/feed/update/urn:li:activity:${activityId}`

